WEBVTT

00:00:19.784 --> 00:00:25.825
Cheedeb presents American Folk Tales by George Gibson.

00:00:25.885 --> 00:00:51.654
The Legend of Johnny Appleseed Part 1.

00:00:51.714 --> 00:01:02.100
Johnny and his apple seeds Johnny apple seed was born near Boston in 1775.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.360
His real name was John Chapman.

00:01:05.421 --> 00:01:10.483
When he was a child, he played in the forest and in the fields.

00:01:10.543 --> 00:01:12.984
His best friends were animals.

00:01:13.044 --> 00:01:15.486
He loved all animals.

00:01:15.546 --> 00:01:18.602
He played with them and talked to them.

00:01:18.662 --> 00:01:21.648
His family was very religious.

00:01:21.708 --> 00:01:24.790
Johnny's first book was the Bible.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:28.522
But he also liked Asop's fables.

00:01:28.582 --> 00:01:33.414
Johnny loved the tales about animals and their adventures.

00:01:33.474 --> 00:01:39.358
When Johnny was a teenager, he worked as a missionary with the Indians.

00:01:39.418 --> 00:01:43.350
He converted many Indians to Christianity.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.192
He taught them about the Bible.

00:01:46.252 --> 00:01:49.322
The Indians were his friends.

00:01:49.382 --> 00:01:54.214
When he was 26 years old, he had a vision.

00:01:54.274 --> 00:01:56.656
An angel appeared to him.

00:01:56.716 --> 00:02:02.320
The angel said, Go and plant apple seeds across America.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:07.364
The settlers of the new frontier want good apples to eat.

00:02:07.423 --> 00:02:10.996
Johnny was surprised, but he was happy.

00:02:11.056 --> 00:02:15.628
He was a kind person, and he wanted to help others.

00:02:15.688 --> 00:02:19.952
He took a big sack and filled it with apple seeds.

00:02:20.012 --> 00:02:22.884
He carried this sack on his back.

00:02:22.944 --> 00:02:29.756
In one hand he carried the Bible, Asop's fables, and other religious books.

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:37.288
Now he was ready to cross the continent and plant America's favorite fruit, the apple.

00:02:37.348 --> 00:02:40.070
Johnny was an unusual man.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.340
He was tall and thin.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.311
He had long hair and a beard.

00:02:45.371 --> 00:02:48.178
He never bought new clothes.

00:02:48.238 --> 00:02:53.347
He wore an old coffee sack and the old clothes people gave him.

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:56.538
He didn't usually wear any shoes.

00:02:56.598 --> 00:02:59.680
He wore a saucepan on his head.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.542
One of Johnny's friends said, God bless you, Johnny.

00:03:04.602 --> 00:03:06.874
We are happy for you.

00:03:06.933 --> 00:03:10.305
You are similar to St. Francis of Assisi.

00:03:10.365 --> 00:03:14.177
He loved animals and lived a simple life.

00:03:14.237 --> 00:03:19.955
Johnny said, I want to plant apple seeds across America.

00:03:20.015 --> 00:03:26.527
Every American family will have apple trees with good apples to eat.

00:03:26.587 --> 00:03:32.599
In 1800, Johnny began his long journey across America.

00:03:32.659 --> 00:03:36.580
At that time, America was a very young country.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.962
The American continent was a wilderness.

00:03:40.022 --> 00:03:42.079
It was unexplored.

00:03:42.138 --> 00:03:45.476
There were no roads and few maps.

00:03:45.536 --> 00:03:50.178
This immense land was called the American Frontier.

00:03:50.238 --> 00:03:54.370
Many settlers wanted to explore the frontier.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.512
Johnny walked from Massachusetts to New York.

00:03:58.572 --> 00:04:01.983
From New York, he walked to Pennsylvania.

00:04:02.043 --> 00:04:07.745
Then he crossed Ohio, Indiana, and a big part of the Midwest.

00:04:07.805 --> 00:04:10.740
Every day he moved west.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.715
He traveled across America and planted apple seeds.

00:04:15.775 --> 00:04:21.047
He built fences around the fields and then continued his journey.

00:04:21.107 --> 00:04:26.430
Settlers traveled to the frontier and found the apple orchards.

00:04:26.489 --> 00:04:32.162
They ate the delicious fruit, green, red and yellow apples.

00:04:32.222 --> 00:04:37.250
When the settlers found an apple orchard, they built a home there.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:42.278
Other settlers dug up the apple trees and took them to New Lands.

00:04:42.338 --> 00:04:48.263
Some of Johnny's trees traveled to the West Coast on the Pacific Ocean.

00:04:48.322 --> 00:04:54.496
When Johnny found a family of settlers, he visited their log cabin.

00:04:54.556 --> 00:04:57.348
He helped them with their work.

00:04:57.408 --> 00:05:01.690
He told the children's stories and sang songs.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:07.468
One day, Johnny visited a family of settlers in the Midwest.

00:05:07.528 --> 00:05:10.682
This family loved books.

00:05:10.742 --> 00:05:14.444
He gave them a few pages from his books.

00:05:14.504 --> 00:05:20.155
You can read them and give them to me when I return in a few months, he said.

00:05:20.215 --> 00:05:23.146
The family was very happy.

00:05:23.206 --> 00:05:29.097
In this way, Johnny created the first library on the frontier.

00:05:29.157 --> 00:05:36.220
Many children learned to read thanks to Johnny and his library.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:41.724
American food made with apples.

00:05:41.784 --> 00:05:46.775
Americans eat a lot of green, yellow, and red apples.

00:05:46.835 --> 00:05:50.486
There is a lot of food and drink made with apples.

00:05:50.546 --> 00:05:57.197
Here is a proverb, an apple a day keeps the doctor away.

00:05:57.257 --> 00:06:02.346
Apples are a delicious fruit and are very good for you.

00:06:02.406 --> 00:06:10.288
Today, in the United States, there are more than 7,000 different kinds of apples.

00:06:10.348 --> 00:06:17.854
At Halloween, children in America bob for apples.

00:06:17.914 --> 00:06:21.406
Part 2.

00:06:21.466 --> 00:06:25.299
Johnny becomes a legend.

00:06:25.359 --> 00:06:32.052
For many years, Johnny walked thousands of miles across the frontier.

00:06:32.112 --> 00:06:40.562
He planted apple seeds, lent books, protected animals, and made friends with settlers and Indians.

00:06:40.622 --> 00:06:46.954
The Indians liked Johnny because he had no weapons and respected nature.

00:06:47.013 --> 00:06:51.524
Johnny learned to speak the languages of many tribes.

00:06:51.584 --> 00:06:57.065
One summer morning, many Indians arrived at a frontier village.

00:06:57.125 --> 00:07:01.939
The Indians wanted to destroy the village and kill the settlers.

00:07:01.998 --> 00:07:08.336
The settlers wanted to send a messenger to a military fort to ask for help.

00:07:08.396 --> 00:07:11.348
The fort was 30 miles away.

00:07:11.408 --> 00:07:13.600
It was very dangerous.

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:16.814
There were enemy Indians everywhere.

00:07:16.874 --> 00:07:20.796
Johnny wanted to save the lives of the settlers.

00:07:20.856 --> 00:07:25.420
He knew all the secret paths in the forest and in the mountains.

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:27.792
He was not afraid.

00:07:27.852 --> 00:07:30.729
He took the message to the fort.

00:07:30.789 --> 00:07:37.049
The soldiers at the fort got on their horses and went to defend the settlers.

00:07:37.109 --> 00:07:42.214
A few years later, Johnny visited an Indian village.

00:07:42.274 --> 00:07:48.180
He heard the Indian chief say, Many settlers live near the river.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.758
Tonight we will kill all of them.

00:07:51.818 --> 00:08:03.321
That evening Johnny ran to the home of every settler and said, The Indians will attack you tonight, run away, go and hide in the forest.

00:08:03.381 --> 00:08:07.994
The settlers escaped to the forest and no one was killed.

00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:12.338
Johnny loved all forms of human and animal life.

00:08:12.398 --> 00:08:16.670
He didn't eat meat because he didn't want to kill animals.

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:19.028
He loved insects too.

00:08:19.088 --> 00:08:22.609
He loved and respected every living thing.

00:08:22.669 --> 00:08:25.820
He was a very special person.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:30.631
One autumn day, Johnny was near an apple orchard.

00:08:30.691 --> 00:08:33.411
He heard the cry of an animal.

00:08:33.472 --> 00:08:36.952
Behind a tree, he found a deer.

00:08:37.012 --> 00:08:40.513
My poor friend said Johnny.

00:08:40.573 --> 00:08:42.193
Don't be afraid.

00:08:42.253 --> 00:08:47.605
He examined the deer and said, Your leg is injured.

00:08:47.665 --> 00:08:49.286
I can help you.

00:08:49.346 --> 00:08:51.918
The deer wasn't afraid.

00:08:51.978 --> 00:08:56.552
Johnny stayed with the deer for many days and helped it.

00:08:56.612 --> 00:08:59.213
I'm happy you can walk again.

00:08:59.273 --> 00:09:02.516
Go and run in the forest, my little friend.

00:09:02.576 --> 00:09:07.919
During a snowstorm, Johnny wanted to sleep inside a small cave.

00:09:07.979 --> 00:09:12.652
He saw a big family of raccoons sleeping inside the cave.

00:09:12.712 --> 00:09:20.238
He did not want the raccoons to go outside into the snow, so Johnny slept outside in the cold.

00:09:20.298 --> 00:09:26.752
For almost 50 years, Johnny Appleseed helped the American frontier to grow.

00:09:26.812 --> 00:09:35.488
His apple trees, his books, his generosity and his kindness made the frontier a happy place.

00:09:35.548 --> 00:09:39.222
Johnny became a legend during his life.

00:09:39.281 --> 00:09:41.286
Everyone loved him.

00:09:41.346 --> 00:09:46.684
He was a true friend of the settlers, the Indians and the animals.

00:09:46.744 --> 00:09:51.478
In America today, people remember him with admiration.

00:09:51.538 --> 00:10:03.107
When Americans eat an apple, they often think of Johnny Appleseed, who made apple trees grow all over America.

00:10:03.167 --> 00:10:09.627
Young America and its settlers.

00:10:09.687 --> 00:10:17.696
When Johnny Appleseed was born in 1775, America was not a nation.

00:10:17.756 --> 00:10:20.378
It was an English colony.

00:10:20.438 --> 00:10:25.642
America became a nation after the American Revolution.

00:10:25.702 --> 00:10:30.674
The enormous American continent was a wilderness.

00:10:30.734 --> 00:10:37.759
There were unexplored forests, mountains, rivers, lakes, fields, and deserts.

00:10:37.819 --> 00:10:43.754
Only the American Indians lived in small parts of this wilderness.

00:10:43.814 --> 00:10:50.198
The American army sent its soldiers to build forts in the wilderness.

00:10:50.258 --> 00:10:53.952
These forts were similar to small villages.

00:10:54.012 --> 00:10:57.934
They protected settlers from Indian attacks.

00:10:57.994 --> 00:11:01.146
They also sold supplies.

00:11:01.206 --> 00:11:07.159
Settlers often built their log cabins near military forts.

00:11:07.219 --> 00:11:12.050
Thousands of settlers wanted to live in these new lands.

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:14.041
It was their dream.

00:11:14.101 --> 00:11:17.222
The settlers didn't usually buy the land.

00:11:17.282 --> 00:11:20.074
It was free from the government.

00:11:20.134 --> 00:11:24.256
Families of settlers traveled in covered wagons.

00:11:24.316 --> 00:11:29.829
These covered wagon trains traveled long distances to new lands.

00:11:29.889 --> 00:11:33.150
Settlers were strong, courageous people.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:35.979
Life on the frontier was not easy.

00:11:36.039 --> 00:11:39.546
It was difficult and dangerous.

00:11:39.606 --> 00:11:44.048
The men built log cabins and hunted for food.

00:11:44.108 --> 00:11:47.468
The women and children worked as farmers.

00:11:47.528 --> 00:11:50.179
There was little free time.

00:11:50.239 --> 00:11:52.680
The settlers were happy.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:56.541
They loved the freedom and adventure of the frontier.

00:11:56.601 --> 00:12:02.940
They built America.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:10.309
Bill and the coyotes.

00:12:10.369 --> 00:12:17.071
Pecos Bill was born in the east of the United States in the 1800s.

00:12:17.131 --> 00:12:20.102
He had a lot of brothers and sisters.

00:12:20.162 --> 00:12:23.533
Bill was the baby of the family.

00:12:23.593 --> 00:12:30.845
When Bill was about two years old, his mother and father decided to move to the west.

00:12:30.905 --> 00:12:33.596
They wanted to be pioneers.

00:12:33.656 --> 00:12:37.132
They liked the adventure of the frontier.

00:12:37.192 --> 00:12:43.510
One day they put all their possessions in a covered wagon and began their journey.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:48.891
The family crossed forests, mountains, rivers and plains.

00:12:48.951 --> 00:12:51.212
They saw many new things.

00:12:51.272 --> 00:12:54.122
They met friendly Indians.

00:12:54.182 --> 00:13:01.064
When they arrived in Texas, little Bill fell out of the covered wagon.

00:13:01.124 --> 00:13:04.655
His brothers and sisters didn't see him fall out.

00:13:04.715 --> 00:13:07.894
His parents didn't see him fall out.

00:13:07.954 --> 00:13:11.665
That evening, his parents looked for him.

00:13:11.725 --> 00:13:16.478
They looked everywhere, but they did not find Little Bill.

00:13:16.538 --> 00:13:21.552
They were very sad, but they continued their journey.

00:13:21.612 --> 00:13:27.285
Little Bill was all alone in the plains of South Texas.

00:13:27.345 --> 00:13:29.766
He was an intelligent child.

00:13:29.826 --> 00:13:31.377
He looked around.

00:13:31.438 --> 00:13:36.050
He saw mountains, cacti, and other small plants.

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:38.515
It was very hot.

00:13:38.575 --> 00:13:41.320
Then he saw a cave.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:45.284
He went inside the cave and slept.

00:13:45.344 --> 00:13:48.486
He slept for a long time.

00:13:48.546 --> 00:13:52.820
In the cave there was a family of coyotes.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:57.174
There was a mother coyote and five small coyotes.

00:13:57.234 --> 00:14:00.176
The mother coyote liked little Bill.

00:14:00.236 --> 00:14:02.658
She decided to protect him.

00:14:02.719 --> 00:14:07.042
Bill liked his new mother and his new brothers and sisters.

00:14:07.102 --> 00:14:10.133
He played with the little coyotes.

00:14:10.193 --> 00:14:13.624
The coyote family was kind to him.

00:14:13.684 --> 00:14:18.495
He copied the coyotes and learned to run and eat.

00:14:18.555 --> 00:14:22.226
He learned to drink water from the river.

00:14:22.286 --> 00:14:27.348
At night, Bill howled at the moon with the coyotes.

00:14:27.408 --> 00:14:31.038
Bill learned to speak the language of the animals.

00:14:31.098 --> 00:14:34.669
Soon Bill forgot about his human family.

00:14:34.729 --> 00:14:37.642
He thought he was a coyote.

00:14:37.702 --> 00:14:42.364
Many years passed, and Bill grew up.

00:14:42.424 --> 00:14:50.246
One day, when he was about 20 years old, Bill was at the Pecos River with the other coyotes.

00:14:50.306 --> 00:14:53.066
He drank water with them.

00:14:53.126 --> 00:14:59.068
A cowboy saw him and asked, Why are you drinking water in that way?

00:14:59.128 --> 00:15:01.068
You aren't a coyote.

00:15:01.128 --> 00:15:07.309
Bill looked at the cowboy and said, Yes, I am.

00:15:07.369 --> 00:15:09.750
No, you're not a coyote.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:11.601
Said the cowboy.

00:15:11.661 --> 00:15:14.504
Of course I'm a coyote.

00:15:14.564 --> 00:15:16.665
This is my coyote family.

00:15:16.725 --> 00:15:17.666
Said Bill.

00:15:17.726 --> 00:15:19.477
Where's your tail?

00:15:19.537 --> 00:15:21.889
Ask the cowboy.

00:15:21.949 --> 00:15:27.494
Bill looked in the water of the Pecos River to see his reflection.

00:15:27.554 --> 00:15:30.006
He didn't see his tail.

00:15:30.066 --> 00:15:32.108
He looked and looked.

00:15:32.168 --> 00:15:34.740
He turned around and looked again.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:36.712
He was surprised.

00:15:36.772 --> 00:15:38.723
He didn't have a tail.

00:15:38.783 --> 00:15:40.534
You are right.

00:15:40.594 --> 00:15:42.766
I'm not a coyote.

00:15:42.826 --> 00:15:44.927
My name is Bill.

00:15:44.987 --> 00:15:46.718
I'm a human.

00:15:46.778 --> 00:15:50.570
This is a big surprise for me, he said.

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:56.064
The cowboy laughed, and Bill laughed too.

00:15:56.124 --> 00:16:01.397
My name's Tall Tong, and I'm a very tall cowboy.

00:16:01.457 --> 00:16:06.277
This is the Pecos River, and I will call you Pecos Bill.

00:16:06.337 --> 00:16:08.654
Come with me, Peko's Bill.

00:16:08.714 --> 00:16:10.725
You can be a cowboy with me.

00:16:10.785 --> 00:16:13.786
We can work together at the Longhorn Ranch.

00:16:13.846 --> 00:16:15.527
What's a cowboy?

00:16:15.587 --> 00:16:17.368
asked Peko's Bill.

00:16:17.428 --> 00:16:21.519
A cowboy watches and guards the cattle and horses.

00:16:21.579 --> 00:16:24.520
He takes cattle from Texas to other places.

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:27.041
People from the East Bay are cattle.

00:16:27.101 --> 00:16:28.871
They like good meat.

00:16:28.931 --> 00:16:32.782
A cowboy is a strong, courageous man.

00:16:32.842 --> 00:16:36.524
Peko's Bill wanted to be a cowboy.

00:16:36.584 --> 00:16:39.536
He said goodbye to the coyotes.

00:16:39.596 --> 00:16:41.527
He was sad to leave them.

00:16:41.587 --> 00:16:43.398
They were his friends.

00:16:43.458 --> 00:16:48.010
Then he said to Tall Tom, let's go to the ranch.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:52.342
The cowboy gave Pecos Bill some cowboy clothes.

00:16:52.402 --> 00:16:56.113
Pecos Bill looked at the clothes and laughed.

00:16:56.173 --> 00:16:58.724
Then he put them on.

00:16:58.784 --> 00:17:01.645
Tall Tom rode his horse.

00:17:01.705 --> 00:17:06.337
Pecos Bill walked near him because he didn't have a horse.

00:17:06.397 --> 00:17:13.663
It was a sunny day, the sky was blue and the sun was hot.

00:17:13.723 --> 00:17:17.437
Part 2.

00:17:17.497 --> 00:17:25.824
King of the Cowboys Pecos Bill and Tall Tom traveled under the hot sun.

00:17:25.885 --> 00:17:28.056
It was a long journey.

00:17:28.117 --> 00:17:32.051
When they arrived at the mountains, it was evening.

00:17:32.111 --> 00:17:35.914
Pecos Bill said, I'm tired.

00:17:35.974 --> 00:17:38.026
Let's sit down and rest.

00:17:38.086 --> 00:17:39.738
That's a good idea.

00:17:39.798 --> 00:17:41.228
Said tall Tom.

00:17:41.288 --> 00:17:43.840
My horse is tired and thirsty.

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:47.344
He gave his horse some water to drink.

00:17:47.404 --> 00:17:52.507
Pecos Bill and tall Tom sat down under a big tree.

00:17:52.567 --> 00:17:55.400
There was a black bird in the tree.

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:57.933
It sang a happy song.

00:17:57.993 --> 00:18:03.496
Pecos Bill knew the language of the animals, so he spoke to the Blackbird.

00:18:03.556 --> 00:18:06.456
They had a long conversation.

00:18:06.516 --> 00:18:12.308
Pecos Bill and Tall Tom ate some biscuits and drank some water.

00:18:12.368 --> 00:18:16.528
Pecos Bill gave the Blackbird some of his biscuit.

00:18:16.588 --> 00:18:24.042
Tall Tom sang a western song, but remember the Red River Valley.

00:18:24.102 --> 00:18:28.736
And the one that has loved you so true.

00:18:28.796 --> 00:18:33.979
Pecos Bill liked it and asked, what's the name of the song?

00:18:34.039 --> 00:18:36.481
It's called Red River Valley.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:40.614
Many cowboys in Texas sing it, said Tall Tom.

00:18:40.674 --> 00:18:45.596
Tall Tom made a fire and they talked about a cowboy's life.

00:18:45.656 --> 00:18:49.038
They looked at the stars in the night sky.

00:18:49.098 --> 00:18:51.154
Then they fell asleep.

00:18:51.214 --> 00:18:56.262
Early the next morning, they began their journey to the ranch.

00:18:56.322 --> 00:19:00.786
After two days, they finally arrived at the cattle ranch.

00:19:00.846 --> 00:19:05.038
The other cowboys were happy to meet Pecos Bill.

00:19:05.098 --> 00:19:10.184
Welcome to Long Horn Range, said the cowboys.

00:19:10.244 --> 00:19:12.526
The ranch was very big.

00:19:12.586 --> 00:19:15.488
There were long horned cattle everywhere.

00:19:15.548 --> 00:19:19.243
They ate grass and drank water at the river.

00:19:19.303 --> 00:19:23.248
Pecos Bill saw the cowboys on their horses.

00:19:23.308 --> 00:19:27.042
Every cowboy had a long rope in his hands.

00:19:27.102 --> 00:19:31.366
I want to be a cowboy, Pecos Bill said to tall Tom.

00:19:31.426 --> 00:19:33.327
What must I do?

00:19:33.388 --> 00:19:36.250
First, you must find a horse.

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:39.162
Second, you must have a rope.

00:19:39.222 --> 00:19:43.626
Then we must take all the cattle to the Red River Valley.

00:19:43.686 --> 00:19:45.991
We must sell the cattle there.

00:19:46.052 --> 00:19:51.718
The Red River Valley is far away, and it is a long, difficult journey.

00:19:51.778 --> 00:19:53.968
Pekas Bill looked around.

00:19:54.028 --> 00:19:57.580
He saw a black horse near a cactus.

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:00.642
No one wanted to ride that horse.

00:20:00.702 --> 00:20:05.694
He went to the horse and talked to it in animal language.

00:20:05.754 --> 00:20:08.694
The horse didn't answer.

00:20:08.754 --> 00:20:11.796
Pekas Bill got on the horse.

00:20:11.856 --> 00:20:14.056
He tried to ride it.

00:20:14.116 --> 00:20:18.488
After a few moments, the black horse bucked him off.

00:20:18.548 --> 00:20:20.810
Peko's bill tried again.

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:24.132
The black horse bucked him off again.

00:20:24.192 --> 00:20:27.794
You're a bucking Bronco.

00:20:27.854 --> 00:20:30.056
That's your new name.

00:20:30.116 --> 00:20:32.138
Bucking Bronco.

00:20:32.198 --> 00:20:35.040
Said Peko's bill to the horse.

00:20:35.100 --> 00:20:40.664
Bucking Bronco bucked Peko's bill off for three days.

00:20:40.724 --> 00:20:43.023
He did not want a master.

00:20:43.083 --> 00:20:46.282
Pecos Bill was strong and determined.

00:20:46.342 --> 00:20:48.824
He wasn't afraid of Buckingham Bronco.

00:20:48.884 --> 00:20:51.746
He wasn't afraid of anything.

00:20:51.806 --> 00:20:57.340
Buckingham Bronco was a beautiful horse, but he was very wild.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:02.194
On the fourth day, Buckingham Bronco stopped Buckingham.

00:21:02.254 --> 00:21:06.086
He understood that Pecos Bill was a special cowboy.

00:21:06.146 --> 00:21:08.991
Pecos Bill was very strong.

00:21:09.052 --> 00:21:14.667
Bucking Bronco knew that Pecos Bill was his new master.

00:21:14.727 --> 00:21:19.129
Pecos Bill and Bucking Bronco became good friends.

00:21:19.189 --> 00:21:22.560
Together they roped the cattle of Texas.

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:26.972
Together they took cattle from Texas to other states.

00:21:27.032 --> 00:21:30.324
Pecos Bill became a famous cowboy.

00:21:30.384 --> 00:21:33.606
He was the best cowboy at the rodeos.

00:21:33.666 --> 00:21:35.926
Everyone knew him and liked him.

00:21:35.986 --> 00:21:40.018
He became the king of the cowboys of Texas.

00:21:40.078 --> 00:21:46.252
After a few years, Pecos Bill met a beautiful woman called Sue.

00:21:46.312 --> 00:21:48.804
She was very friendly.

00:21:48.864 --> 00:21:52.656
Her home was near the Rio Grande River.

00:21:52.716 --> 00:21:56.608
Pecos Bill loved her and she loved him.

00:21:56.668 --> 00:22:00.040
One sunny day in April, they got married.

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:04.168
Everyone at the Longhorn Ranch celebrated the wedding.

00:22:04.228 --> 00:22:06.735
There was an exciting rodeo.

00:22:06.795 --> 00:22:11.438
There was music, dancing, and a lot of good food.

00:22:11.498 --> 00:22:18.302
Supesam, the friendly cook, organized a pie-eating competition.

00:22:18.362 --> 00:22:22.754
Tall Tom's favorite food was apple pie.

00:22:22.814 --> 00:22:25.455
But he did not win the competition.

00:22:25.515 --> 00:22:29.628
His friend Big Bob won the pie-eating competition.

00:22:29.688 --> 00:22:34.184
Big Bob ate 88 apple pies.

00:22:34.244 --> 00:22:40.902
Pecos Bill and Sue were very happy at the Longhorn Ranch.

00:22:40.962 --> 00:22:50.430
Pecos Bill never forgot his friends the coyotes, and he never forgot the language of the animals.

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:59.916
Life on a Ranch Cowboys usually lived and worked on a ranch.

00:22:59.976 --> 00:23:03.577
A ranch was a very big piece of land.

00:23:03.638 --> 00:23:07.737
There was usually only one owner of a ranch.

00:23:07.797 --> 00:23:12.299
There were cattle, sheep, and horses on a ranch.

00:23:12.359 --> 00:23:15.930
Rodeos were a favorite pastime.

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:21.492
When cowboys were at the ranch, they lived together in a bunk house.

00:23:21.552 --> 00:23:25.554
The owner of the ranch lived in another house.

00:23:25.614 --> 00:23:29.385
The cowboys ate together in a big room.

00:23:29.445 --> 00:23:32.521
Cowboys had big appetites.

00:23:32.582 --> 00:23:35.868
The cook was an important person.

00:23:35.928 --> 00:23:41.612
Cowboys often moved cattle to another place to sell them.

00:23:41.672 --> 00:23:46.964
10 or 12 cowboys moved about 3,000 cattle.

00:23:47.024 --> 00:23:49.296
This was difficult work.

00:23:49.356 --> 00:23:52.928
They usually traveled for many weeks.

00:23:52.988 --> 00:23:56.990
They lived on the plains and in the mountains.

00:23:57.050 --> 00:24:00.574
They cooked their meals on an open fire.

00:24:00.634 --> 00:24:03.538
They slept under the stars.

00:24:03.598 --> 00:24:09.350
Sometimes it was very hot, and other times it rained or snowed.

00:24:09.410 --> 00:24:14.734
Sometimes during the long journey, there were Indian attacks.

00:24:14.794 --> 00:24:19.476
When the cowboys sold the cattle, they were very happy.

00:24:19.536 --> 00:24:22.857
They stayed in a town for a few days.

00:24:22.918 --> 00:24:25.238
They bought new things.

00:24:25.298 --> 00:24:28.256
They went to the town saloon.

00:24:28.316 --> 00:24:33.923
At the saloon they played cards, drank whiskey and had a bath.

00:24:33.983 --> 00:24:37.104
Then they returned to their ranch.

00:24:37.164 --> 00:24:41.816
Today there are many cattle ranches in the United States.

00:24:41.876 --> 00:24:46.607
These ranches are in Texas and in the western states.

00:24:46.667 --> 00:24:53.648
Cowboys work on these ranches and they still ride horses.

00:24:53.708 --> 00:25:07.396
The Tale of Brayer Rabbit and the Tar Baby Part 1 The Tar Baby It was a hot day in August.

00:25:07.456 --> 00:25:12.368
Summer is a very hot season in the South of the United States.

00:25:12.428 --> 00:25:17.750
All the animals on the old plantation had a rest.

00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:21.966
Brayer Fox was outside his house.

00:25:22.026 --> 00:25:27.851
He sat under a magnolia tree and drank cold lemonade.

00:25:27.911 --> 00:25:30.492
He was very hot.

00:25:30.552 --> 00:25:34.194
He was also angry and nervous.

00:25:34.254 --> 00:25:38.057
Brere Fox didn't like Brere Rabbit.

00:25:38.117 --> 00:25:45.112
Before Brere Rabbit came to the old plantation, Brere Fox was a happy fox.

00:25:45.172 --> 00:25:48.894
The old plantation was a peaceful place.

00:25:48.954 --> 00:25:51.746
Brere Rabbit tricked everyone.

00:25:51.806 --> 00:25:58.718
He tricked Bear Bear, Bear Turtle, Bear Wolf, and Bear Fox.

00:25:58.778 --> 00:26:02.479
Bear Rabbit was a very intelligent rabbit.

00:26:02.539 --> 00:26:05.750
He was young and dressed well.

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:09.221
Bear Fox was tired of Bear Rabbit.

00:26:09.281 --> 00:26:11.441
He decided to trick him.

00:26:11.501 --> 00:26:16.102
He went to his garden and took a big bucket of tar.

00:26:16.162 --> 00:26:19.418
He put other oils in the bucket too.

00:26:19.478 --> 00:26:22.924
Then he mixed the tar for a long time.

00:26:22.984 --> 00:26:25.526
The tar had a terrible odor.

00:26:25.586 --> 00:26:27.887
It was very sticky.

00:26:27.947 --> 00:26:31.198
Bear Fox went into his house.

00:26:31.259 --> 00:26:37.192
He went to the kitchen to look for an old straw hat, but he didn't find it.

00:26:37.252 --> 00:26:39.672
Then he went to the living room.

00:26:39.732 --> 00:26:41.933
He looked there too.

00:26:41.994 --> 00:26:44.985
Finally he went to the bedroom.

00:26:45.045 --> 00:26:50.716
In the bedroom he found an old straw hat, two buttons and a comb.

00:26:50.776 --> 00:26:53.047
He put them in a sack.

00:26:53.107 --> 00:26:57.698
Then he took the bucket of tar and walked to the road.

00:26:57.758 --> 00:27:03.890
He threw the tar near a log and made a big black tar baby.

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:07.680
He put the old straw hat on the tar baby.

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:11.071
He put on two buttons for the eyes.

00:27:11.131 --> 00:27:14.527
Then he put on the comb for the mouth.

00:27:14.587 --> 00:27:18.263
Brere Fox looked at his work and was happy.

00:27:18.323 --> 00:27:20.774
The tar baby was ready.

00:27:20.834 --> 00:27:24.315
Brere Fox hid behind a big tree.

00:27:24.375 --> 00:27:27.496
He waited for Brere Rabbit to walk by.

00:27:27.556 --> 00:27:29.517
He waited and waited.

00:27:29.577 --> 00:27:32.638
It was very hot.

00:27:32.698 --> 00:27:37.849
After an hour, Brere Rabbit walked down the road.

00:27:37.909 --> 00:27:39.590
He was very happy.

00:27:39.650 --> 00:27:43.643
He walked, jumped and sang a song.

00:27:43.703 --> 00:27:47.868
Brere Fox watched him from behind the tree.

00:27:47.928 --> 00:27:51.500
Suddenly, Brere Rabbit saw the tar baby.

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:53.882
He stopped and looked at it.

00:27:53.942 --> 00:27:56.584
Brere Rabbit was a friendly rabbit.

00:27:56.644 --> 00:27:59.607
He said, good morning.

00:27:59.667 --> 00:28:02.138
It's a hot day today.

00:28:02.198 --> 00:28:06.222
The tar baby smiled but didn't answer.

00:28:06.282 --> 00:28:09.274
I am from the old plantation.

00:28:09.335 --> 00:28:10.746
Said Brere Rabbit.

00:28:10.806 --> 00:28:12.514
Where are you from?

00:28:12.574 --> 00:28:16.034
The tar baby smiled but didn't answer.

00:28:16.094 --> 00:28:20.038
Brere Fox watched everything from behind the tree.

00:28:20.098 --> 00:28:22.720
He wanted to laugh but he didn't.

00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:25.432
Brere Rabbit tried again.

00:28:25.492 --> 00:28:27.214
Good morning.

00:28:27.274 --> 00:28:28.744
How are you?

00:28:28.804 --> 00:28:32.688
The tar baby smiled but didn't answer.

00:28:32.748 --> 00:28:35.350
What are you doing here?

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:37.021
Where are you from?

00:28:37.082 --> 00:28:39.364
Brere Rabbit asked again.

00:28:39.424 --> 00:28:42.919
The tar baby smiled but didn't answer.

00:28:42.979 --> 00:28:45.063
Brad Rabbit was angry.

00:28:45.123 --> 00:28:47.846
His face and ears were red.

00:28:47.906 --> 00:28:49.576
Can you hear me?

00:28:49.596 --> 00:28:53.049
I said, good morning.

00:28:53.109 --> 00:28:55.020
Why don't you answer me?

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:56.262
He shouted.

00:28:56.322 --> 00:28:58.192
There was no answer.

00:28:58.252 --> 00:29:01.264
Brad Rabbit was very angry.

00:29:01.324 --> 00:29:04.678
You are very unfriendly.

00:29:04.738 --> 00:29:06.488
I'm a friendly rabbit.

00:29:06.548 --> 00:29:08.360
I want to be your friend.

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:09.871
Who are you?

00:29:09.931 --> 00:29:12.428
The tar baby didn't answer.

00:29:12.488 --> 00:29:14.834
Brad Rabbit was furious.

00:29:14.894 --> 00:29:16.706
He hit the tar baby.

00:29:16.766 --> 00:29:20.588
His front paw was stuck in the tar baby's face.

00:29:20.648 --> 00:29:23.060
Brad Fox was very happy.

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:24.842
He laughed quietly.

00:29:24.902 --> 00:29:26.192
Let me go!

00:29:26.252 --> 00:29:28.054
Said Brad Rabbit.

00:29:28.114 --> 00:29:30.656
The tar baby did not let go.

00:29:30.716 --> 00:29:32.417
Let me go!

00:29:32.477 --> 00:29:34.348
Said Brad Rabbit again.

00:29:34.408 --> 00:29:36.880
Brad Rabbit kicked the tar baby.

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:40.856
Now his back paw was stuck in the tar baby's body.

00:29:40.916 --> 00:29:43.829
Please, let me go!

00:29:43.889 --> 00:29:45.649
Shouted Bear Rabbit.

00:29:45.709 --> 00:29:48.240
He kicked the tar baby again.

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:51.030
Now his other back pole was stuck.

00:29:51.090 --> 00:29:52.291
Help!

00:29:52.351 --> 00:29:53.841
I can't move!

00:29:53.901 --> 00:29:54.731
He shouted.

00:29:54.792 --> 00:29:56.642
This is terrible!

00:29:56.702 --> 00:29:58.402
Poor Bear Rabbit.

00:29:58.462 --> 00:30:00.453
He was covered with tar.

00:30:00.513 --> 00:30:03.664
His face and ears were black with tar.

00:30:03.724 --> 00:30:05.964
His paws were black with tar.

00:30:06.024 --> 00:30:10.699
He was a very unhappy rabbit.

00:30:10.759 --> 00:30:17.146
Exercise 6, page 67.

00:30:17.206 --> 00:30:23.781
Listen to the text and circle the words that are mentioned in each room.

00:30:23.841 --> 00:30:28.004
Let's look at Brayer Fox Manor.

00:30:28.064 --> 00:30:31.317
First, let's go to the living room.

00:30:31.376 --> 00:30:36.920
In the living room, there are books and there are two chairs.

00:30:36.981 --> 00:30:39.805
Now let's go to the kitchen.

00:30:39.865 --> 00:30:43.588
In the kitchen, there's a table and a chair.

00:30:43.648 --> 00:30:46.708
There's a cup on the table.

00:30:46.769 --> 00:30:49.699
Now let's go to the bedroom.

00:30:49.759 --> 00:30:56.650
In the bedroom, there's a bed, a clock and a jacket.

00:30:56.710 --> 00:31:00.691
Fox hunting.

00:31:00.751 --> 00:31:04.642
In Great Britain, fox hunting is permitted.

00:31:04.702 --> 00:31:08.944
But many British people think it is cruel to hunt foxes.

00:31:09.004 --> 00:31:13.169
They want a law to stop fox hunting.

00:31:13.229 --> 00:31:19.974
In some states of the USA, fox hunting is still permitted.

00:31:20.034 --> 00:31:23.356
There are different types of foxes.

00:31:23.416 --> 00:31:26.608
The most popular is the Red Fox.

00:31:26.668 --> 00:31:29.110
It lives in Europe.

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:35.256
The San Joaquin Kit Fox lives in the North American deserts.

00:31:35.316 --> 00:31:39.318
In the Arctic, we find the White Arctic Fox.

00:31:39.378 --> 00:31:45.400
The Fennec Fox is very small and it has big ears.

00:31:45.460 --> 00:31:52.282
Today people want to help and protect animals, especially young animals.

00:31:52.342 --> 00:32:02.706
The WWF World Wildlife Fund defends animals all over the world.

00:32:02.766 --> 00:32:05.998
Part 2.

00:32:06.058 --> 00:32:16.227
The Briar Patch When Bear Fox saw Bear Rabbit covered with tar, he laughed and laughed.

00:32:16.267 --> 00:32:22.592
He walked down the road and said, This is a good lesson for you, Bear Rabbit.

00:32:22.652 --> 00:32:26.665
You always tricked everyone on the old plantation.

00:32:26.725 --> 00:32:28.957
This time I tricked you.

00:32:28.977 --> 00:32:32.289
This is the end of Bear Rabbit.

00:32:32.349 --> 00:32:37.099
Bear Fox looked at Bear Rabbit and laughed again.

00:32:37.159 --> 00:32:41.227
Bear Rabbit didn't say one word.

00:32:41.287 --> 00:32:42.607
He was frightened.

00:32:42.667 --> 00:32:44.208
He didn't move.

00:32:44.268 --> 00:32:51.970
Bear Fox looked at his watch and said, it's dinner time and I'm very hungry.

00:32:52.030 --> 00:32:55.460
I want Rabbit BBQ for dinner.

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:58.791
Rabbit BBQ is delicious.

00:32:58.851 --> 00:33:02.332
I must find some wood to make a fire.

00:33:02.392 --> 00:33:05.587
Bear Fox went to look for some wood.

00:33:05.648 --> 00:33:08.233
The Brere Rabbit started to think.

00:33:08.293 --> 00:33:11.264
He was a very intelligent rabbit.

00:33:11.324 --> 00:33:14.496
His eyes moved from left to right.

00:33:14.556 --> 00:33:16.606
He looked everywhere.

00:33:16.666 --> 00:33:19.367
Then he saw a Brere Patch.

00:33:19.428 --> 00:33:23.079
The Brere Patch can take off the tar.

00:33:23.139 --> 00:33:25.080
But I can't move.

00:33:25.140 --> 00:33:26.660
I'm stuck.

00:33:26.721 --> 00:33:29.642
I must go to the Brere Patch.

00:33:29.702 --> 00:33:31.462
What can I do?

00:33:31.522 --> 00:33:33.409
Thought Brere Rabbit.

00:33:33.469 --> 00:33:39.928
Brere Fox returned and said, I didn't find any wood to make a fire.

00:33:39.988 --> 00:33:46.412
I can't have rabbit barbecue for dinner, but I can hang ya.

00:33:46.472 --> 00:33:53.856
Oh, Brere Fox, you can hang me, but please don't throw me in the Brere patch.

00:33:53.916 --> 00:33:55.586
Said Brere, Rabbit.

00:33:55.646 --> 00:33:59.409
Brere Fox looked for a rope.

00:33:59.469 --> 00:34:01.526
There's no rope.

00:34:01.586 --> 00:34:03.654
I can't hang you.

00:34:03.714 --> 00:34:05.986
How can I kill you?"

00:34:06.046 --> 00:34:07.837
asked Brirfox.

00:34:07.898 --> 00:34:14.933
He thought for a moment and said, I can throw you in a river or a lake.

00:34:14.994 --> 00:34:19.097
Oh Brirfox, throw me in a river or a lake.

00:34:19.157 --> 00:34:23.520
But please don't throw me in the terrible briar patch.

00:34:23.580 --> 00:34:25.372
Please!

00:34:25.433 --> 00:34:29.246
Brere Fox went to look for a river or a lake.

00:34:29.307 --> 00:34:33.481
He looked everywhere, but he didn't find a river or a lake.

00:34:33.541 --> 00:34:35.173
He was angry.

00:34:35.234 --> 00:34:39.621
He wanted to kill Brere Rabbit, but how?

00:34:39.682 --> 00:34:43.057
Have you got a heart, Rare Fox?

00:34:43.116 --> 00:34:47.419
Please, don't throw me into the Briar Patch.

00:34:47.479 --> 00:34:48.849
Said Briar Rabbit.

00:34:48.910 --> 00:34:51.081
Oh, please.

00:34:51.141 --> 00:34:52.331
Well.

00:34:52.392 --> 00:34:54.423
Said Briar Fox smiling.

00:34:54.482 --> 00:34:58.405
You don't want to go into the Briar Patch.

00:34:58.465 --> 00:35:01.947
That's exactly where I will throw you.

00:35:02.007 --> 00:35:04.719
Into the Breyer Patch.

00:35:04.779 --> 00:35:08.761
Breyer Fox threw Breyer Rabbit into the Breyer Patch.

00:35:08.821 --> 00:35:11.853
This was exactly what Breyer Rabbit wanted.

00:35:11.913 --> 00:35:16.896
The tar baby stuck to the Breyer Patch and Breyer Rabbit was free.

00:35:16.956 --> 00:35:24.245
When Breyer Fox saw the tar baby in the Breyer Patch, he asked, What's happening?

00:35:24.305 --> 00:35:27.450
Where is that cunning rabbit?

00:35:27.510 --> 00:35:30.402
Why is the tar baby here?

00:35:30.462 --> 00:35:34.654
Brayer Rabbit ran up the road and then stopped.

00:35:34.714 --> 00:35:40.318
He looked at Brayer Fox and said, you didn't listen to me.

00:35:40.378 --> 00:35:45.331
I said, please don't throw me into the briar patch.

00:35:45.391 --> 00:35:48.373
Next time you'll listen to me.

00:35:48.433 --> 00:35:52.540
Brayer Rabbit laughed and ran home to have a bath.

00:35:52.600 --> 00:35:55.856
You horrible cunning rabbit!

00:35:55.916 --> 00:35:58.437
You tricked me again!"

00:35:58.497 --> 00:36:00.217
shouted Bear Fox.

00:36:00.278 --> 00:36:02.328
He was purple with anger.

00:36:02.388 --> 00:36:06.429
He looked at the briar patch and he looked at the tar baby.

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:08.750
Then he walked home slowly.

00:36:08.810 --> 00:36:11.341
He was very sad and angry.

00:36:11.401 --> 00:36:13.842
Bear Rabbit tricked him again.

00:36:13.902 --> 00:36:17.303
Why was Bear Rabbit so intelligent?

00:36:17.362 --> 00:36:23.326
When Brere Fox went into his garden, he sat down under the magnolia tree.

00:36:23.386 --> 00:36:24.877
He was tired.

00:36:24.938 --> 00:36:27.150
He looked at the evening sky.

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:33.294
He saw the stars and the moon and fell asleep.

00:36:33.354 --> 00:36:44.409
Brere Tails and the South Welcome to the South, and welcome to the old plantation.

00:36:44.469 --> 00:36:48.950
The people of the South are famous for their hospitality.

00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:55.542
Brere tales were told about 200 years ago on the plantations of the South.

00:36:55.602 --> 00:37:01.714
The children of the plantations listened to these tales about funny animal characters.

00:37:01.774 --> 00:37:05.057
Everyone loved Brere tales.

00:37:05.117 --> 00:37:12.983
In 1880, the American writer Joel Chandler Harris published these tales.

00:37:13.043 --> 00:37:19.566
His book was called Uncle Remus, his songs and sayings.

00:37:19.626 --> 00:37:24.308
Harris created a character named Uncle Remus.

00:37:24.368 --> 00:37:28.960
Uncle Remus told the tales and everyone liked him.

00:37:29.020 --> 00:37:34.534
There is also a film about Uncle Remus and his tales.

00:37:34.594 --> 00:37:42.500
In the 1700s and 1800s, there were many plantations in the south.

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:50.214
Tobacco, cotton, and sugar cane were the most important products of the plantations.

00:37:50.274 --> 00:37:55.708
Every plantation had a big, beautiful house and garden.

00:37:55.768 --> 00:37:59.179
The owners of the plantation lived there.

00:37:59.240 --> 00:38:04.913
Today, in the south of the United States, you can visit old plantation houses.

